WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.497
Hello! Welcome to the "Math Experiments".

00:00:03.497 --> 00:00:06.441
I've been wanting to talk about the distribution of prime numbers for a while.

00:00:06.441 --> 00:00:08.940
How we can study this distribution

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.515
and what connection it has with random processes.

00:00:12.515 --> 00:00:14.868
and, of course, with the Riemann hypothesis.

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:17.202
So...

00:00:17.202 --> 00:00:19.466
How can we study prime numbers?

00:00:19.466 --> 00:00:23.580
For example, we can study the sums of powers of first prime numbers.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.852
For instance, the sum of the 1 powers, that is just sum of primes.

00:00:26.852 --> 00:00:36.632
Here we get pairs of number P 
 and the sum of all prime numbers from 1 to P inclusive.

00:00:36.632 --> 00:00:43.689
We can study the sum of the first powers or, for instance, inverse powers, or zero powers.

00:00:43.689 --> 00:00:46.297
When you calculate  sums of zero power of prime numbers,

00:00:46.297 --> 00:00:52.024
you are, in fact, just counting prime numbers less than x.

00:00:52.024 --> 00:00:54.579
Let's start with this...

00:00:54.579 --> 00:01:01.611
It is common to start with this prime counting function.

00:01:01.611 --> 00:01:04.052
And we will do that too.

00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:10.000
Although this function is quite a die hard.

00:01:14.022 --> 00:01:19.413
Here I computed the pi function by hand.

00:01:19.413 --> 00:01:25.093
The fact is that this function is already defined in Wolfram. 

00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:27.950
It is called PrimePi. No need to define it by hand.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:33.998
And it is amazing that it can be computed
even for quite large  x values.

00:01:33.998 --> 00:01:39.024
Let's draw a plot of this function for x from 0 to 70.

00:01:39.024 --> 00:01:40.224
Here it is...

00:01:40.224 --> 00:01:43.546
For example, for 60 we have...

00:01:43.546 --> 00:01:48.628
17 or 18 prime numbers less 60

00:01:48.628 --> 00:01:58.749
Here I wanted to show, what I can compute this
using Accumulate[primes⁰],

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:04.194
it gives the same thing as the PrimePi function.

00:02:04.194 --> 00:02:08.401
How to explore PrimePi function?

00:02:08.401 --> 00:02:16.250
Let's start by calculating proportion of prime numbers among all numbers ≤ x changes.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:20.452
PrimePi[x] / x.

00:02:20.452 --> 00:02:23.744
Here we see a decreasing function,

00:02:23.744 --> 00:02:28.621
and around a million it is approximately 0.08.

00:02:28.621 --> 00:02:36.082
So, 8% of the all integer numbers less than 1 million are prime.

00:02:36.082 --> 00:02:40.654
And the function continues to decrease further.

00:02:40.654 --> 00:02:47.125
Lets make it visible and draw this plot on a logarithmic axis.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:52.779
Here I simply took the variable lx from 2 to 20

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:56.834
and put the exponential of lx inside the function f.

00:02:58.155 --> 00:03:05.226
The further away, the closer this function to zero,

00:03:05.226 --> 00:03:11.487
and around e³⁰ the proportion of prime numbers is already less than 5%.

00:03:11.487 --> 00:03:19.473
Next, we can come up with a formula for this decreasing function.

00:03:19.473 --> 00:03:23.587
This formula is 1/Log[x]

00:03:23.587 --> 00:03:27.127
It is just experienced eye. Or two or three guesses, and you find this answer.

00:03:27.127 --> 00:03:30.154
An easy part.

00:03:30.154 --> 00:03:35.735
So, now I am considering PrimePi[x]/x/Log[x]

00:03:35.735 --> 00:03:41.663
and I see that gradually this plot approaches one.

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:46.474
Good. This is our first theorem.

00:03:46.474 --> 00:03:49.853
Not a theorem, but discovered experimental fact.

00:03:49.853 --> 00:03:54.921
Let's consider the difference PrimePi[x] - x/(Log[x] - a)

00:03:54.921 --> 00:04:00.361
And here we add one more parameter "a" to the denominator.

00:04:01.712 --> 00:04:04.006
Let's see what we can observe.

00:04:04.006 --> 00:04:11.814
Here we can see that by changing the parameter "a", we can reduce this difference.

00:04:12.753 --> 00:04:20.153
For small values of "a", this difference reaches 3000 and more.

00:04:20.153 --> 00:04:29.317
And when we increase the parameter "a", 
 gradually we start to see something interesting.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:43.237
We can see that for a = 1.086, we can reduce this difference almost to zero.

00:04:43.237 --> 00:04:48.525
Well, not exactly zero, ofc, but to numbers like 60, 20.

00:04:48.525 --> 00:04:51.669
On the interval x ≤ 1000000.

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:58.517
Let's remember this value as the initial value for the parameter "a".

00:04:58.517 --> 00:05:02.492
And let's increase our range of x.

00:05:02.492 --> 00:05:04.441
And what do we see?

00:05:04.441 --> 00:05:08.232
We see that...

00:05:08.232 --> 00:05:15.484
the good behavior that we saw for small values 
 of x is significantly distorted for this parameter.

00:05:15.484 --> 00:05:24.896
If we want to get something good for big x, this parameter needs to be smaller.

00:05:24.896 --> 00:05:28.716
For example, like this.

00:05:28.716 --> 00:05:32.393
Need more time to draw...

00:05:43.784 --> 00:05:50.302
If we leave the parameter at 1.065, then as we further increase x, 
 we will see this bend again

00:05:50.302 --> 00:05:54.034
and the function will again go down to a negative value.

00:05:55.091 --> 00:06:00.174
The optimal parameter is probably around 1.05 or even smaller.

00:06:00.174 --> 00:06:05.961
Then the function becomes steadily increasing, but nothing to worry about.

00:06:05.961 --> 00:06:10.205
Let's find a law for this steadily increasing function.

00:06:10.205 --> 00:06:12.264
So! There is idea:

00:06:12.264 --> 00:06:17.755
PrimePi has a main component – x/(log[x]-a)

00:06:17.755 --> 00:06:26.915
and we need to find a law for the residue or,
i.e. mismatch between the pi-function and the  approximation.

00:06:26.915 --> 00:06:33.210
It is natural to assume that this law is a power law.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:44.311
And here I have already adjusted the sliders 
 and found some values for the multiplier and for the power "c".

00:06:45.926 --> 00:06:52.613
"b" is  just  the multiplier in front of the power law.

00:06:52.613 --> 00:06:56.165
And "c" is precisely the power.

00:06:57.046 --> 00:07:01.141
When c = 0.5 we get a square root,

00:07:01.141 --> 00:07:05.284
but we need something more than a square root here.

00:07:07.662 --> 00:07:10.750
Let's assume it's something like this.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:15.157
Here I am already taking the difference:

00:07:15.157 --> 00:07:16.629
Between the Pi function,

00:07:16.629 --> 00:07:17.836
the main component,

00:07:17.836 --> 00:07:21.334
and the second component, which we just found.

00:07:21.334 --> 00:07:25.066
From the values of b and c from the previous adjustment.

00:07:25.066 --> 00:07:27.066
And what do we see here?

00:07:27.066 --> 00:07:31.356
We see a very important thing here - noise.

00:07:31.356 --> 00:07:34.563
Noise that pleases my eye.

00:07:34.563 --> 00:07:39.293
It grows and needs to be divided so that it does not grow.

00:07:39.293 --> 00:07:42.878
For example, by √x

00:07:42.878 --> 00:07:43.837
Here.

00:07:43.837 --> 00:07:49.946
Now it no longer grows.
Its amplitude even decreases slightly with increasing x.

00:07:49.946 --> 00:07:51.996
We will fix this in the future.

00:07:51.996 --> 00:07:59.176
Let's assume that we have found such a law and it suits us.

00:07:59.176 --> 00:08:02.351
Let's write it down.

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:07.466
Pi minus the main component,

00:08:07.466 --> 00:08:09.853
minus the second component,

00:08:09.853 --> 00:08:11.472
and the constant is not so important here...

00:08:11.472 --> 00:08:15.879
Dividing by the square root of x - that's noise.

00:08:15.879 --> 00:08:20.668
And if we express Pi from this formula, here's what we get:

00:08:20.668 --> 00:08:30.232
Pi equals: the main component + the second component + √n times the noise

00:08:31.583 --> 00:08:35.397
This is what you can get in the first ten minutes of studying the Pi function.

00:08:35.397 --> 00:08:37.544
This is the wrong answer.

00:08:38.836 --> 00:08:42.568
It's hard to get the right answer by just moving sliders.

00:08:42.568 --> 00:08:44.098
You have to think more...

00:08:44.098 --> 00:08:47.948
Think and use a different method.

00:08:49.181 --> 00:08:53.627
Namely - the fitting method.

00:08:53.627 --> 00:08:58.533
We assume that our dependence has this form.

00:08:58.533 --> 00:09:01.912
And here I clearly wrote down the noise.

00:09:01.912 --> 00:09:05.465
And allowed it to be shifted by some "b".

00:09:05.465 --> 00:09:08.520
Added a constant "e".

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.612
Here are our constants "c" and "d" for the second component

00:09:11.612 --> 00:09:16.254
and constant "a" for the first component.

00:09:16.254 --> 00:09:24.481
Then I take and express noise through Pi and everything else.

00:09:24.481 --> 00:09:32.229
And define the Noise function, which depends on PrimePi.

00:09:32.229 --> 00:09:38.307
Then I want to minimize this noise.

00:09:38.307 --> 00:09:42.685
Find such parameters a, b, c, d, e, so that the noise is as small as possible.

00:09:44.711 --> 00:09:47.443
Here I use the QRange function,

00:09:47.443 --> 00:09:55.731
which generates a list of natural numbers located approximately on 
 a geometric progression up to the first argument.

00:09:55.731 --> 00:10:00.000
The number of these numbers is determined by the second argument.

00:10:01.551 --> 00:10:08.797
Here I have defined the QRange function.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.393
So.

00:10:12.393 --> 00:10:20.499
I'm starting to minimize the mean value of squared noise

00:10:20.499 --> 00:10:34.033
at points located approximately in geometric progression 
 from 10 to 500,000,000,010.

00:10:34.033 --> 00:10:41.464
I ask the NMinimize function to use the simulated annealing method
and here's the answer it gives me:

00:10:41.473 --> 00:10:46.785
The parameter "a" -&gt; 1.02, we had 1.05, remember, right?

00:10:47.695 --> 00:10:53.746
And it chose this power for the second component: d -&gt; 0.78

00:10:53.746 --> 00:11:01.196
Let's define the function PrimePiMy according 
 to this approximation

00:11:01.196 --> 00:11:04.722
that we obtained with these parameters.

00:11:04.722 --> 00:11:13.721
And let's plot the discrepancy between the function PrimePiMy 
 and the true function PrimePi.

00:11:13.721 --> 00:11:21.853
Let's divide by √x, because, as we know, this difference grows as x increases.

00:11:21.853 --> 00:11:31.251
Yes, and here we see that when we divide by √x, 
 this noise decreases.

00:11:31.251 --> 00:11:41.324
Actually, we need to multiply by the logarithm 
 so that this noise doesn't decrease in amplitude.

00:11:41.324 --> 00:11:48.137
And here I specifically chose 5 trillion as the final number.

00:11:48.137 --> 00:11:54.570
That is, I was optimizing for 500 billion.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:59.065
And for 500 billion, I get this.

00:11:59.065 --> 00:12:02.499
And when I look at what's happening on the right.

00:12:02.499 --> 00:12:07.258
I see what the extrapolation of what I picked looks like,

00:12:07.258 --> 00:12:16.099
I see that it's getting worse and worse over there. 
 You can't even see how bad it's getting beyond that.

00:12:16.774 --> 00:12:19.623
So what do I do?

00:12:19.623 --> 00:12:23.103
The first thing I do is fix it.

00:12:23.103 --> 00:12:32.853
My noise grows not like √n, but like √n/Log[n].

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:38.716
I choose this expression and see what the noise looks like.

00:12:38.716 --> 00:12:46.192
I define Noise according to this expression.

00:12:47.161 --> 00:12:51.275
My Noise depends on PrimePi and the parameters a, b, c, d, e.

00:12:51.275 --> 00:12:54.692
And again, I repeat the whole process.

00:12:54.692 --> 00:12:57.544
Again, I ask to use the Simulated Annealing method.

00:12:57.544 --> 00:13:00.189
There are other interesting methods there too.

00:13:00.189 --> 00:13:04.131
For example, I recommend NelderMead.

00:13:04.131 --> 00:13:08.871
And this is also a good method: DifferentialEvolution.

00:13:09.634 --> 00:13:12.167
The answer it found is like this.

00:13:12.167 --> 00:13:18.219
Very similar, again "a" -&gt; 1.029 and again "d" is close to 0.8.

00:13:19.247 --> 00:13:23.073
I define again my function PrimePi

00:13:23.073 --> 00:13:28.889
and look at the difference between the interpolation up to 500 billion

00:13:28.889 --> 00:13:33.323
and then I look at the extrapolation.

00:13:33.323 --> 00:13:36.596
The situation is actually the same.

00:13:36.596 --> 00:13:41.620
Not much has changed. Essentially, we have the same answer here

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:48.199
because we haven't done anything new. We just fixed the noise amplitude error.

00:13:48.199 --> 00:13:49.853
Here's another approach.

00:13:49.853 --> 00:13:56.816
Let's assume that we need to add not " nᵈ " to the main component,

00:13:56.816 --> 00:14:02.896
but this thing: " n " divided by some higher powers of the logarithm.

00:14:04.066 --> 00:14:06.917
And the noise is still √n/Log[n].

00:14:06.917 --> 00:14:09.853
This seems to be the correct estimate of the noise amplitude.

00:14:09.853 --> 00:14:10.741
Let's go.

00:14:11.857 --> 00:14:19.800
We get an expression. And here I have already rewritten it, replacing Pi with PrimePi.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:20.622
Okay.

00:14:20.622 --> 00:14:25.294
Again we minimize the noise.

00:14:26.293 --> 00:14:33.027
This time my error is already 0.049, while before it was 0.07.

00:14:33.027 --> 00:14:35.198
So the error decreased.

00:14:35.198 --> 00:14:44.654
This is the criterion that the new parametric formula 
 for a, b3, b4, b5 is better than the previous one.

00:14:44.654 --> 00:14:48.591
The average error has decreased.

00:14:48.591 --> 00:14:54.056
We define the function PrimePiMy and plot...

00:14:54.056 --> 00:14:59.109
We plot the noise value:
PrimePiMy[#] - PrimePi[#]

00:14:59.109 --> 00:15:02.078
Here it is! This is what I like.

00:15:02.078 --> 00:15:04.519
Look how good everything looks.

00:15:04.519 --> 00:15:09.396
There is no more of that sine wave onto which the noise is superimposed.

00:15:09.396 --> 00:15:15.115
And it doesn't go up as much as before.

00:15:15.115 --> 00:15:18.029
Here I can stop bothering about that approximation is not ideal:

00:15:18.029 --> 00:15:26.987
Well, I just took some finite number of powers of the logarithm, 
 and I could take some more terms,

00:15:26.987 --> 00:15:31.953
and fix the bend at the right side.

00:15:31.953 --> 00:15:38.891
The most important thing here is that there is no more of that sine wave, those additional waves.

00:15:38.891 --> 00:15:42.156
Then I realize that I'm doing something silly.

00:15:42.156 --> 00:15:47.855
This first component, if it is expanded in powers of 1/Log[n],

00:15:47.855 --> 00:15:51.381
then it turns out to be this.

00:15:51.381 --> 00:15:57.049
So in a sense, it's easier to just choose these kinds of terms

00:15:57.049 --> 00:16:00.957
rather than trying to make the first term something special.

00:16:01.691 --> 00:16:03.649
Let me explain.

00:16:03.649 --> 00:16:12.579
Instead of this and instead of the first term, 
 which of course becomes much better when you add "-a" to it,

00:16:12.579 --> 00:16:24.287
you can take this function, 
 bring n/Log[n] out of the parentheses, and just add all the powers of 1/Log[n].

00:16:24.287 --> 00:16:26.850
And then add noise again.

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:29.295
See how Noise is expressed

00:16:29.295 --> 00:16:33.937
and nicely write this expression here.

00:16:34.377 --> 00:16:36.595
Repeat the same thing again.

00:16:36.595 --> 00:16:56.023
And in fact, we don't get anything new because we've just 
 rewritten our idea in a simpler form using powers of 1/Log[n].

00:16:56.023 --> 00:16:59.015
This is what our new approximation equals.

00:16:59.544 --> 00:17:04.266
And let's look at the noise again.

00:17:04.266 --> 00:17:11.932
Add a zero to see the extrapolation up to 5 trillion.

00:17:15.162 --> 00:17:17.762
Here there is a slight increase.

00:17:17.762 --> 00:17:25.311
Most likely because the coefficients are not perfectly chosen
and one can add further powers here.

00:17:25.311 --> 00:17:31.850
Naturally, the question arises,
what function is hidden here in the parentheses?

00:17:32.319 --> 00:17:38.530
The answer is: all this expression actually tends to,

00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:41.177
if you continue this fitting process,

00:17:41.177 --> 00:17:44.439
to the LogIntegral function.

00:17:45.231 --> 00:17:47.730
What is LogIntegral?

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:52.810
It's a function that equals the logarithm of the integral of 1/Log[x].

00:17:54.366 --> 00:18:01.764
More precisely, it equals the integral from 0 to y of the function LogIntegral [y],

00:18:01.764 --> 00:18:06.344
but we need to deal with the division by zero at x=1.

00:18:06.344 --> 00:18:12.953
To do this, we subtract this pole (1/(x-1)) from the function.

00:18:12.953 --> 00:18:19.338
And, accordingly,  we need to subtract the logarithm from the answer.

00:18:19.824 --> 00:18:26.846
This kind of integration of a function with a pole in the middle is called 
 a principal value integral.

00:18:26.846 --> 00:18:37.164
Here we just surround the problematic point x=1 with a small interval and exclude it from the integration.

00:18:42.742 --> 00:18:46.502
LogIntegral is a very unusual function.

00:18:46.502 --> 00:18:52.553
You see, when I ask Mathematica to expand this function in powers of x,

00:18:52.553 --> 00:19:03.361
it says that it is equal to x/Log[x]⁹, 
 and then there is a polynomial of the ninth degree of Log[x].

00:19:03.361 --> 00:19:13.755
Actually, if we will do this multiplication, do Normal // Expand,

00:19:13.755 --> 00:19:20.399
then we will see that LogIntegral contains x/Log[x] as the main component,

00:19:20.399 --> 00:19:27.067
and then these terms, where the power of the logarithm in the denominator grows.

00:19:27.067 --> 00:19:29.067
Perfectly.

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:38.689
And, by the way, we can see what the coefficients are equal to: 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, 720... 
 this is n factorial (n!).

00:19:38.689 --> 00:19:41.746
And this is a scary dependence, of course.

00:19:41.746 --> 00:19:46.414
It is clear that if we take a lot of terms,

00:19:46.414 --> 00:19:56.077
then at some point the next term will be greater and greater 
 and exceed one in modulus.

00:19:56.077 --> 00:20:01.011
This is the same case when we should not fully expand this series,

00:20:01.011 --> 00:20:08.060
because it diverges if we take an infinite number of its terms.

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:14.192
Here, I'm showing this fact again.

00:20:14.192 --> 00:20:17.601
I divided LogIntegral[x] by x

00:20:17.601 --> 00:20:21.920
to see only the component about the logarithms of x.

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:28.823
Or, this is how we can see all of this and make sure that 
 we really have n-factorial in the numerator.

00:20:28.823 --> 00:20:36.907
And what's drawn here is madness.
Such a convergent series cannot exist.

00:20:36.907 --> 00:20:37.967
Okay.

00:20:37.967 --> 00:20:49.093
Up there, what were the coefficients? 
 Let's look at them carefully.

00:20:49.093 --> 00:20:51.702
1, 1,

00:20:51.702 --> 00:20:54.403
1.6 (it was 2 there).

00:20:54.403 --> 00:20:57.372
Here we have 14, and there it was 6.

00:20:57.372 --> 00:21:04.949
Here we have 27, and there it was 24.

00:21:12.994 --> 00:21:20.000
I'm telling you, we need to take these coefficients
to get a good approximation of the PrimePi function.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:25.670
Let's try to summarize what we have:

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:32.577
First: the noise amplitude equals √x/Log[x]

00:21:32.577 --> 00:21:40.272
and the first approximation is x/(Log[x]-a).

00:21:40.272 --> 00:21:42.947
The second approximation is...

00:21:44.751 --> 00:21:49.344
Let me write it down so it's in front of us.

00:21:49.344 --> 00:21:50.963
The second approximation is this approximation,

00:21:50.963 --> 00:21:55.605
in which the main component is x/Log[x] * 1

00:21:55.605 --> 00:21:59.269
and there are these additional components.

00:21:59.269 --> 00:22:01.813
And we can ignore this.

00:22:01.813 --> 00:22:09.273
This component is much smaller than this quantity,
because here we have the square root of x, and here we have x.

00:22:10.594 --> 00:22:15.801
And the next approximation is the LogIntegral.

00:22:15.801 --> 00:22:21.118
The first one is the blue. You see, it goes up.

00:22:21.118 --> 00:22:27.257
The second one is my approximation. The yellow one.

00:22:27.257 --> 00:22:33.680
And the third one is the LogIntegral approximation. The green.

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:40.260
You can see that the yellow one is the best of these three in this range of values.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:41.259
But...

00:22:41.933 --> 00:22:46.927
But in reality, this approximation can be greatly improved.

00:22:46.927 --> 00:22:50.633
And let me do that right now.

00:22:50.633 --> 00:23:04.701
We can add the LogIntegral of the square root of x with a coefficient of 1/2.
"1/2LogIntegral[Sqrt[x]]"

00:23:04.701 --> 00:23:10.753
Let's see how much the green graph improves.

00:23:11.253 --> 00:23:12.916
Here we go!

00:23:15.764 --> 00:23:25.389
Here we can add 1/3 of the LogIntegral of the cube root of x and get an even better green graph. "1/3LogIntegral[Sqrt[x,3]]"

00:23:25.389 --> 00:23:30.480
Riemann obtained a more accurate formula through LogIntegral.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:32.098
Here's what it looks like:

00:23:32.098 --> 00:23:39.556
You have to take the sum of LogIntegals of this expression multiplied by mu[i]/i.

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:45.442
These things have to be taken with different coefficients.

00:23:45.442 --> 00:23:49.260
This is the main, first component for i=1.

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:53.409
Then comes this component, which I have already written above.

00:23:53.409 --> 00:23:56.698
Then there's 1/3 also with a minus sign.

00:23:56.698 --> 00:24:02.044
Then there's 1/5 (we're going by prime numbers) also with a minus sign.

00:24:02.044 --> 00:24:03.105
Skipping ahead...

00:24:03.105 --> 00:24:05.439
Next is 1/7, also with a minus sign.

00:24:05.439 --> 00:24:09.180
Basically, all prime numbers here have a minus sign,

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:13.997
while the product of two prime numbers has a plus sign.

00:24:13.997 --> 00:24:19.462
For example, 1/14 has a plus sign.

00:24:19.462 --> 00:24:22.340
Numbers that contain a square of a prime number,

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:24.432
where the decomposition includes the square of some prime number,

00:24:24.432 --> 00:24:26.050
are absent here.

00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:29.075
For them, the MoebiusMu function is equal to zero.

00:24:29.075 --> 00:24:35.425
The MoebiusMu function is equal to 1, -1, or 0.

00:24:35.425 --> 00:24:42.270
It is equal to zero for numbers not free from squares, 
 inside of which there is a square of a prime number.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:53.035
And it is equal to 1 or -1, 
 depending on how many prime numbers need to be multiplied to obtain i.

00:24:56.025 --> 00:25:00.000
So this is the first component of R [x, n].

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:07.872
LogIntegral and also such things.
They are also LogIntegral only from the roots of x and with coefficients.

00:25:07.872 --> 00:25:12.661
The most important component in R [x, n] is this one.

00:25:12.661 --> 00:25:20.970
This component is not that important.
All other components are only important for small values of x.

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:28.484
That is, everything else is needed to converge better for small x.

00:25:28.484 --> 00:25:36.120
For the asymptotic, only this component is important.

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:41.111
This addition is even smaller than all these additions.

00:25:41.111 --> 00:25:51.478
It is of the order of magnitude of 1/Log [x]² without the x multiplier on top.

00:25:51.478 --> 00:26:04.041
Here you can see on the LogLogPlot,
that the decrease is approximately quadratic, 1/Log [x]².

00:26:04.041 --> 00:26:11.274
So, let me define this Riemann function

00:26:11.274 --> 00:26:19.027
and draw four noise plots.

00:26:19.027 --> 00:26:22.326
Pi minus the first - the simplest approximation.

00:26:22.326 --> 00:26:28.671
Pi minus my approximation, where there are powers of n/Log[x]ᵏ.

00:26:28.671 --> 00:26:32.241
LogIntegral approximation.

00:26:32.241 --> 00:26:38.446
And this is the most accurate Riemann approximation.

00:26:38.446 --> 00:26:43.587
The first approximation, the blue one, goes up.

00:26:43.587 --> 00:26:46.465
The yellow and the green...

00:26:46.465 --> 00:26:54.099
The yellow approximation, here it is, it's not visible here, 
 it's hidden behind the red one.

00:26:54.099 --> 00:26:57.009
Hidden behind the back of the red one.

00:26:57.009 --> 00:27:03.299
This approximation - this is the green LogIntegral approximation.

00:27:03.299 --> 00:27:06.452
Yes, we need to add 1/2  here
(UPDATE: not 1/2, but 1)

00:27:06.452 --> 00:27:22.062
 Or maybe, as I said earlier, add 1/2LogIntegral[Sqrt[x]] here.

00:27:22.062 --> 00:27:28.316
Here, we will see how the green approximation will become better.

00:27:28.316 --> 00:27:29.338
Here we go!

00:27:30.718 --> 00:27:35.027
So all three approximations are equally good

00:27:35.027 --> 00:27:41.265
and approximately after 10⁹ they are practically indistinguishable.

00:27:41.265 --> 00:27:49.279
For common goals, 
 this approximation can be used perfectly well.

00:27:49.279 --> 00:27:56.406
It is good after x = 100 and up to x ≈ 10¹¹.

00:27:56.406 --> 00:28:04.059
Here I drew my approximation minus the superior 
 Riemann approximation.

00:28:04.059 --> 00:28:11.285
And you can see that mine approximation is not so good below 100 and above 10¹¹.

00:28:11.285 --> 00:28:17.686
But below 100, you can just remember the tabulated function PrimePi[x].

00:28:17.686 --> 00:28:22.769
And above 10¹¹, it might not be interesting to anyone.

00:28:22.769 --> 00:28:32.313
At such large values of x, the answer is unlikely to be needed by anyone.

00:28:32.313 --> 00:28:34.108
Takeaways.

00:28:34.108 --> 00:28:34.753
First:

00:28:34.753 --> 00:28:40.860
The prime-counting function Pi grows roughly as x/Log[x],

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:44.798
and this limit is equal to one.

00:28:44.798 --> 00:28:49.682
We have quickly arrived at a very important and interesting thing.

00:28:49.682 --> 00:29:03.631
The noise that manifests if we subtract all the analytical components 
 we find in the Pi function.

00:29:03.631 --> 00:29:09.466
We understand that this noise grows roughly as x/Log[x],

00:29:09.466 --> 00:29:12.641
and here's what it looks like.

00:29:12.641 --> 00:29:20.460
If you take a logarithmic x-axis, it behaves as if it doesn't change

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:24.515
and makes more or less the same noise.

00:29:24.515 --> 00:29:29.883
And of course, physicists can attack it and study it, as they do

00:29:29.883 --> 00:29:32.411
using spectral analysis,

00:29:32.411 --> 00:29:36.814
using some autocorrelation functions of this noise,

00:29:36.814 --> 00:29:41.926
and discover many interesting facts about prime numbers.

00:29:41.926 --> 00:29:47.566
Discover experimentally. Not prove, of course.

00:29:47.566 --> 00:29:51.647
Next. The next important takeaway.

00:29:51.647 --> 00:29:58.512
When you have some conjecture about the analytic form of a function,

00:29:58.512 --> 00:30:05.972
you can use NMinimize to find and adjust the coefficients of 
 your formula.

00:30:07.234 --> 00:30:08.975
Next up.

00:30:08.975 --> 00:30:16.828
A good approximation for the function Pi 
 is the LogIntegral function.

00:30:16.828 --> 00:30:20.208
In its simplest form, it can be written as this equation, 

00:30:20.208 --> 00:30:25.991
but more accurately, it looks like this:

00:30:25.991 --> 00:30:38.090
R[x], which is approximately equal to li[x], and a complex component, 
 which is very, very small and only important for small x.

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:41.440
This component is written here.

00:30:42.379 --> 00:30:43.575
Moving on.

00:30:44.045 --> 00:30:45.590
The most important thing.

00:30:46.412 --> 00:30:51.763
It turns out the following statement is true:

00:30:51.763 --> 00:31:00.928
The fact that this noise is bounded by √x*Log[x] 
 is equivalent to the Riemann hypothesis.

00:31:02.689 --> 00:31:20.248
As a reminder, the Riemann hypothesis is the conjecture that all non-trivial zeros of 
 the ζ-function lie on the vertical line in the complex plane with real part 1/2.

00:31:20.248 --> 00:31:29.031
The ζ[z] function is a function that is defined as an infinite sum for z &gt; 1,

00:31:29.031 --> 00:31:35.229
and as an analytic continuation of this function in all other points.

00:31:35.229 --> 00:31:41.613
As we can see, the noise actually runs in the range from -0.5 to 5.

00:31:41.613 --> 00:31:48.487
But it is possible that sometimes it may jump up to some point in isolated areas.

00:31:48.487 --> 00:31:57.739
It is assumed that individual points in this noise

00:31:57.739 --> 00:32:05.904
run away from the x-axis up and down no more than some constant times log[x]².

00:32:06.873 --> 00:32:20.000
Although, when you look at it, it feels like it's just in some strip, 
 and that the running away doesn't increase with the growth of x.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.264
Perhaps that's the case.

00:32:22.264 --> 00:32:32.764
This is, of course, interesting, if the stronger statement is true that there is a strip that can completely cover this noise from above.

00:32:35.905 --> 00:32:37.574
That's all for today.

00:32:37.574 --> 00:32:40.439
Good luck to everyone. Bye!

